The morning bell rang, and I hurried to my desk, trying not to let my ears twitch at the sound of the air-conditioning humming. To everyone else, I was just another student in uniform, but underneath my blazer, my skin felt too warm, and my eyes caught the light in a way that made people look away.

During lunch, I slipped behind the old banyan tree near the sports field. The roots seemed to hum beneath my fingertips, and for a moment, the noise of the canteen faded into a soft, green silence. I pressed my palm against the bark and whispered a small thanks, the way my grandmother taught me.

Back in class, my friend asked why I always smelled like rain and cedar. I just smiled and said it was my new shampoo. She laughed, but I felt a little guilty. Keeping the secret was getting harder, especially when the biology teacher started talking about photosynthesis and I had to pretend I didn’t already know how it felt to drink sunlight.The afternoon sun slanted through the classroom windows, and I had to fight the urge to stretch my fingers toward the light. My friend nudged me and asked if I was daydreaming again. I nodded, but really I was listening to the faint rustle of leaves outside, like they were calling my name.

After school, I stayed behind to help the librarian shelve books. The old wooden shelves felt warm under my hands, almost alive. When no one was looking, I let my fingertips brush the grain, and a quiet calm settled over me. It was the closest I could get to home without anyone noticing.

Walking home, I passed the construction site where they were cutting down the old trees. My chest tightened. I wanted to shout at them to stop, but I kept walking, head down, pretending to be ordinary. That night, I sat by my window and watched the moon rise, wondering how long I could keep pretending before the forest inside me grew too loud to hide. [end of text]


